The  pseudo‐device  driver  provides an interface to hardware‐as‐
sisted data movers, which the kernel supports using the facility.
This can be used to copy data from one location in memory to  an‐
other,  clear  a region of memory, fill a region of memory with a
pattern, and perform simple operations  on  multiple  regions  of
memory, such as an XOR, without intervention by the CPU.  A func‐
tion  always  has one output region.  A function may have zero or
more input regions, or may use an immediate value  as  an  input.
For  functions which use input regions, the lengths of each input
region and the output region must be  the  same.   All  functions
with  the same name will have the same number of and type inputs.
To use the client must first create a session.  This is  achieved
by  performing  the  following  steps: Create a session handle by
opening the device.  Select the function using  the  DMIO_SETFUNC
ioctl, which takes the following argument: #define DMIO_MAX_FUNC‐
NAME     64 struct dmio_setfunc {
        char  dsf_name[DMIO_MAX_FUNCNAME];  };  If  the specified
function is not available, the DMIO_SETFUNC ioctl will fail  with
an error code of To submit a request for processing the following
steps  must  be  performed:  Fill in a request structure: typedef
struct {
        struct iovec *dmbuf_iov;
        u_int dmbuf_iovcnt; } dmio_buffer;

struct dmio_usrreq {
        /* Output buffer. */
        dmio_buffer req_outbuf;

        /* Input buffer. */
        union {
                uint8_t _immediate[8];
                dmio_buffer *_inbuf;
        } _req_inbuf_un;

#define req_immediate           _req_inbuf_un._immediate  #define
req_inbuf               _req_inbuf_un._inbuf

        uint32_t req_id;        /* request ID; passed in response
*/ }; For functions which use an immediate value as an input, the
member is used to specify the value.  Values smaller than 8 bytes
should  use  the  least‐significant  bytes first.  For example, a
32‐bit integer would occupy bytes 0, 1, 2, and 3.  For  functions
which  use  input regions, should point to an array of The should
be a unique value for each request submitted by the  client.   It
will  be passed back unchanged in the response when processing of
the request has completed.  Write the request  structure  to  the
session  handle  using the system call.  Multiple requests may be
written to the session in  a  single  call.   Read  the  response
structure  back  from  the  session handle using the system call.
The response structure is defined as follows: struct dmio_usrresp
{
        uint32_t resp_id;
        int resp_error; }; The corresponds to the in the request.
contains 0 if the request succeeded or an  value  indicating  why
the  request  failed.   Multiple  responses may be read back in a
single call.  Note that responses may not be received in the same
order as requests were written.  When a client is finished  using
a session, the session is destroyed by closing the session handle
using  The following is an example of a client using to zero‐fill
a region of memory.  In this example, the  application  would  be
able to perform other work while the hardware‐assisted data mover
clears  the  specified  block of memory.  int hw_bzero(void *buf,
size_t len) {         static uint32_t reqid;

        struct  dmio_setfunc  dsf;          struct   iovec   iov;
        struct dmio_usrreq req;         struct dmio_usrresp resp;
        int fd;

        fd  = open("/dev/dmoverio", O_RDWR, 0666);         if (fd
== ‐1)                 return (‐1);

        strcpy(dsf.dsf_name, "zero");

        if   (ioctl(fd,   DMIO_SETFUNC,   &dsf)    ==    ‐1)    {
                close(fd);                 return (‐1);         }

        iov.iov_base = buf;         iov.iov_len = len;

        req.req_outbuf.dmbuf_iov   =  &iov;          req.req_out‐
buf.dmbuf_iovcnt = 1;         req.req_id = reqid++;

        if  (write(fd,  &req,  sizeof(req))  !=  sizeof(req))   {
                close(fd);                 return (‐1);         }

        /* Application can do other work here. */

        if  (read(fd,  &resp,  sizeof(resp))  !=  sizeof(resp)) {
                close(fd);                 return (‐1);         }

        if       (resp.resp_id       !=       req.req_id)       {
                close(fd);                 return (‐1);         }

        if  (resp.resp_error  !=  0) {                 close(fd);
                return (‐1);         }

        close(fd);         return (0); }  The  device  first  ap‐
